WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.300
- Today we have a very special event.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.950
I think this is probably the first time

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.690
in the history of the Yale Council

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.560
of Southeast Asian
Studies Brown Bag Series

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.853
that we have undergraduate speakers.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.000
In fact that we rarely have
graduate students speakers,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.390
but I was so inspired when
I heard about the work

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.080
that is going on by these students

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.470
at the Fulbright University of Vietnam,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.810
in collaboration with
colleagues in Myanmar,

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:38.620
and other organizations
across Southeast Asia,

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:40.460
that it really struck a chord with me

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.460
about the importance of
Inter-Asian students solidarity,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.080
especially in these times.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.050
It's something we often
talk about as scholars

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.573
about the importance of
Inter-Asian research, right?

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.780
But in a moment of
decolonizing the academy,

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.140
rethinking Southeast Asian Studies.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.640
To see students from Vietnam

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.450
who are taking an interest in Myanmar

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:08.290
to me is a signal of
the important directions

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.600
of that scholarship and activism

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.750
and thinking and work needs
to be going in the future.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.850
So I was really excited
to be able to find a slot

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.560
in our Brown Bag to afford the possibility

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.223
for today's event.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.630
The event will be inspired by a Zine,

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:28.000
on life and Post-Coup Myanmar.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.510
That the Phuong Anh and Tam
Nguyen, have been working on

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:34.950
with other collaborators
at Fulbright University

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.493
and with a collaborator in Myanmar.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.840
And they'll be talking about that today,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.070
but let me just say a few
words about who they are.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.590
So Phuong Anh Nguyen
is a history sophomore

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.010
at Fulbright University in Vietnam

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:53.392
whose current interest is in
human rights in Southeast Asia.

00:01:53.392 --> 00:01:56.270
Tam Nguyen is a poet, art writer,

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:59.240
apprentice curator based
in Ho Chi Minh City,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.550
his practice merges, creative
writing, critical theories

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.700
and art history to investigate Vietnamese

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.100
and Southeast Asian modern

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.460
and contemporary cultural spectacles

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.600
in light of neoliberalism
and globalization.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.420
They're gonna talk for about 20 minutes

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.370
about their Zine project.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:20.110
Then after that, we'll also
be joined by Laure Siegel,

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.800
who is a Journalist and
Founder of Visual Rebellion,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.430
a grassroots movement of
creators for creators,

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.170
and they stayed on their website,

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.980
that they believe that
photography, film, and art

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.300
have the power to create
change for the better.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.080
They are dedicated to
helping Burmese creatives

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.570
by providing a platform for their work

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.330
in opposition to the military junta.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.510
Extremely timely and important work.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.603
So I'm really excited to have
Laure joining us here as well.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.580
If internet conditions allow,
we will also have a guest

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.960
who will be going by
the name of Marla Lamay,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.830
hopefully joining when the
internet allows from Myanmar

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.230
and joining in on the conversation.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.000
So the structure of our event

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.570
will basically be for the
first 20 minutes or so

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.160
we'll hear about the Zine.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.780
Then we'll hear a little
bit about Visual Rebellion

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.120
and then we'll quickly turn
it after about half an hour,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.520
a total to open Q and A discussion.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.330
And we really hope that will
be a dynamic conversation

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:27.230
that brings in all kinds of
voices during the conversation.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.960
So without further ado,

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.503
let me turn it over to
Phuong Anh Nguyen and Tam.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:33.893
